INT. GARAGE APARTMENT - AFTERNOON

A converted garage with a low ceiling and one window the size of a cereal box. Cardboard and plastic bags of hardware cover the floor. A half-built bunk bed frame lies on its side like a patient.

LUCIANO FERRARI, forties, sleeves rolled, kneels over an instruction sheet that has no words, only drawings. His wife MARISOL FERRARI, forties, sits cross-legged holding a hex key and a juice box. Their daughter PIP, nine, sorts bolts into a muffin tin. Their son TOMASZ, fifteen, leans on the doorframe with earbuds in one ear.

                    LUCIANO
          Okay, everybody. Step four. Step four is the ladder.

                    MARISOL
          I thought step four was the guard rail.

                    LUCIANO
          Step four is the ladder. Step five is the guard rail.

                    PIP
          Papa, there are two step fours.

He turns the sheet over. Then back.

                    LUCIANO
          (to himself)
          There are two step fours.

                    MARISOL
          Pip, how many bolts do we have of the long kind?

                    PIP
          Eleven. The directions say twelve.

                    TOMASZ
          So we're one short, which means we've been doing it wrong the whole time.

                    LUCIANO
          Tomasz, go outside and breathe.

                    TOMASZ
          I'm literally just standing here.

                    MARISOL
          Tommy, would you hold this end?

He pulls out the earbud and takes the rail. She slides a peg into a hole. It fits with a soft click.

                    MARISOL (CONT'D)
          Look at that.

                    LUCIANO
          (muttering)
          Beginner's luck.

                    PIP
          Papa, I heard that.

                    LUCIANO
          You were supposed to.

He picks up the long bolt he thought was missing from under the instruction sheet and holds it up like a prize.

                    LUCIANO (CONT'D)
          Twelve.

                    PIP
          Told you it was somewhere.

                    TOMASZ
          You said eleven.

                    PIP
          Eleven in the tin. Twelve in the universe.

Marisol laughs into her juice box. Luciano tightens the bolt with the key, a careful quarter turn at a time.

                    LUCIANO
          Everyone hold your end. On three. One, two...

The frame lifts. It stands. It wobbles a little and settles.

                    MARISOL
          Well. It's a bed.

                    TOMASZ
          It's crooked.

                    LUCIANO
          It's a crooked bed. It's ours.

                    PIP
          Who gets the top?

                    TOMASZ
          Obviously me.

                    PIP
          Obviously not.

                    MARISOL
          We'll flip for it later. Right now, who's hungry?

                    LUCIANO
          (to the room)
          Dinner is whoever can find the pot with the lid.

Nobody moves. Pip returns to the muffin tin and pushes the bolts around with one finger.

                    PIP
          (quietly, to the bolts)
          I hope I get the bottom.

Tomasz hears. He says nothing, but he sits on the lower mattress and bounces once, testing it, then pats the top one with his palm as if saying goodnight.

                    TOMASZ
          Fine. I'll take the top. For the exercise.

                    PIP
          Thanks, Tommy.

                    TOMASZ
          Don't make it weird.

Luciano sets down the key and sits on the floor with his back against the frame, looking at the crooked ceiling.

                    LUCIANO
          Marisol. We have a house with a ladder now.

                    MARISOL
          We have a house with two step fours.

She leans her head on his shoulder. The window light slides a few inches across the floor.
